/** \addtogroup area_memory */
/** \defgroup theory_memory Theory of memory
 \ingroup area_memory

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/memory` realize, beyond the book, the
 dynamic storage allocation of section 2.5 of the first volume of Knuth and
 the allocators of today that descend from it: the boundary tags and the
 buddy system on a simulated memory, the arenas and the pools of objects of
 one type, the size classes for one thread and for many, the huge pages,
 and a heap of cells with a garbage collector. This page collects what the
 headers take for granted: the vocabulary of blocks, free lists and
 fragmentation (see \ref theory_memory_notation), the allocators and the
 cost of their operations (see \ref theory_memory_real), and, for each
 family, the invariant on which it rests and the argument behind its cost
 and its waste (from \ref theory_memory_tags to \ref theory_memory_gc). The
 pseudocode of each operation is instead on the page of its header, in the
 comment of its function, together with the algorithm of Knuth, or the
 paper, that it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_memory_notation Definitions and notation

 An allocator manages a memory of \f$ N \f$ words (or bytes) and serves two
 operations: `allocate( n )` gives a <em>block</em> of at least \f$ n \f$
 contiguous words that no other block overlaps, and `free()` (or
 `deallocate()`) takes a block back; the blocks not given are
 <em>free</em>, and an allocator keeps them on one or more <em>free
 lists</em>, which the free blocks themselves hold, a word of a free block
 pointing to the next one (the list AVAIL of Knuth). A block may carry a
 <em>header</em>, words of its own that the allocator reads, e.g., its
 size; a <em>sized</em> deallocation, as that of `std::allocator`, gives the
 size back with the block, so that the block needs no header.

 The memory that an allocator holds and does not give for use is its
 <em>fragmentation</em>. It is <em>internal</em> when a block is larger
 than its request (the rounding to a class, to a power of 2, the header),
 and <em>external</em> when free memory exists in pieces too small for a
 request, so that a request fails, or takes new memory, although the free
 words would be enough. Merging adjacent free blocks (coalescing) fights the
 external fragmentation, and giving each request a block of one of a few
 sizes, a <em>size class</em>, removes it within a class at the price of an
 internal one; the allocators of the area are points between the two.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ N \f$</td>
     <td>the words of a simulated memory, \f$ 2^m \f$ for the buddy
         system</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$, \f$ b \f$, \f$ a \f$</td>
     <td>the words or bytes of a request, and its alignment</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s( p ) \f$</td>
     <td>the words of the block at the address \f$ p \f$, kept in its
         tags</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ F \f$, \f$ F_k \f$, \f$ F_c \f$</td>
     <td>a free list, that of the blocks of \f$ 2^k \f$ words, that of the
         class \f$ c \f$</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ g \f$</td>
     <td>the step of the size classes of the pools, 8 bytes</td></tr>
 <tr><td>page, segment</td>
     <td>the \f$ 2^{16} \f$ bytes of the slots of one class, and the
         \f$ 2^{21} \f$ bytes from which the pages are cut</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_memory_real The allocators and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The allocators of the area and the cost of their
          operations</caption>
 <tr><th>Allocator</th><th>Blocks</th><th>Allocate</th><th>Free</th>
     <th>Waste</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BoundaryTagAllocator</tt></td><td>any size</td>
     <td>\f$ O( f ) \f$ for \f$ f \f$ free blocks</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$,
     merging both neighbors</td><td>2 words of tags, external
     fragmentation</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>BuddyAllocator</tt></td><td>powers of 2</td>
     <td>\f$ O( m ) \f$ splits</td><td>\f$ O( m ) \f$ merges</td>
     <td>less than half of each block</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>Arena</tt></td><td>objects of one type</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, a pointer bump</td><td>only all at once</td>
     <td>part of the last block</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ObjectPool</tt></td><td>objects of one type</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, but for a new block</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>at most the largest size held, plus a block</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SizeClassAllocator</tt></td><td>41 classes up to 32768
     bytes</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, sized</td>
     <td>below 20% past 128 bytes</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>PoolResource</tt>, <tt>PageHeap</tt></td><td>classes of
     8 bytes up to \f$ M \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td><td>below \f$ a \f$ bytes per slot</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ConcurrentPoolResource</tt></td><td>as above, any
     thread</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, a
     compare-and-swap per 32 frees of other threads</td>
     <td>as above, and 31 slots per class and thread in transit</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>HugePageAllocator</tt></td><td>any, aligned to 2 MB from
     2 MB</td><td>that of the system</td><td>that of the system, or kept
     from 8 MB</td><td>address space up to a huge page</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>CellHeap</tt></td><td>cells of two fields</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$, a collection when none is free</td>
     <td>by the collector</td><td>none</td></tr>
 </table>

 The general allocators of section 2.5 serve any size and pay for it with a
 search of the free list and with the external fragmentation; the pools
 serve one size, or a few, and pay with the internal fragmentation and with
 memory that a class keeps when another needs it. The second kind is the
 one of the allocators of today, which add the locality: the blocks made one
 after another are next to each other, and a block just freed, still in the
 cache, is the next one given.

 \section theory_memory_tags Boundary tags

 `BoundaryTagAllocator` keeps the free blocks on a doubly linked list and
 writes the size of each block, and whether it is free, in a tag at both of
 its ends (Algorithm 2.5C of Knuth).

 <em>Lemma 1 (the boundary tags).</em> After each operation the blocks tile
 the memory and
 \f[
   \text{no two free blocks are adjacent.} \tag{1}
 \f]

 A freed block reads the tag just below it, which is the upper tag of the
 block below, and the tag just above it, the lower tag of the block above:
 two reads tell whether its neighbors are free and how large they are, and
 since the list is doubly linked each free neighbor leaves the list in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$, and merges; hence (1) is kept by every free in
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time. An allocation splits a block only if the rest has
 room for the tags and the two links, and gives the upper part, so that the
 lower part keeps its place on the list and only its tags change. The search
 for a block is the cost of the method: the first fit stops at the first
 block large enough, the best fit reads the whole list, and the next fit
 starts where the last search stopped, so that the small pieces left by the
 splits do not gather at the head of the list. Knuth reports, as the
 fifty-percent rule, that in equilibrium the free blocks are about half as
 many as the blocks in use, whatever the fit.

 \section theory_memory_buddy The buddy system

 `BuddyAllocator` (Knowlton; Algorithms 2.5R and 2.5S of Knuth) gives
 blocks of \f$ 2^k \f$ words only, each at an address multiple of its size.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the buddies).</em> The block of \f$ 2^k \f$ words at the
 address \f$ a \f$, \f$ k < m \f$, was split from the block of
 \f$ 2^{k+1} \f$ words at \f$ a \wedge \neg 2^k \f$, and its buddy, the other
 half, is at
 \f[
   a \oplus 2^k . \tag{2}
 \f]

 By induction on the splits: a block of \f$ 2^{k+1} \f$ words at an address
 multiple of \f$ 2^{k+1} \f$ has the bit \f$ k \f$ of its address 0, and its
 halves are at \f$ a \f$ and \f$ a + 2^k \f$, which differ in that bit
 only, and are multiples of \f$ 2^k \f$. A request of \f$ n \f$ words takes
 \f$ k = \lceil \log_2 n \rceil \f$, and splits the least free block not
 smaller, by halves, each upper half joining its free list; a freed block
 finds its buddy by (2) and, if it is free and whole, merges with it and
 goes on upwards. Both take at most \f$ m \f$ steps, each a search of an
 ordered set of the free blocks of one size in the code, i.e.,
 \f$ O( m \log N ) \f$ time; a block is less than twice its request, so
 that the internal waste is less than half of the block, and no two free
 buddies are ever both on the lists.

 \section theory_memory_pools Arenas, pools and size classes

 An <em>arena</em> (Hanson) gives the objects of one type from blocks, by a
 pointer bump, and frees them all at once with the arena; an object costs no
 header and no search, the objects made one after another are next to each
 other, and their addresses never change, which the parents of the nodes of
 a search rely on. A <em>pool</em> (`ObjectPool`) adds the free of one
 object: its slot goes at the head of a free list, the list AVAIL of
 section 2.2.3 of Knuth, and the next allocation takes it again, still in
 the cache. Its blocks double from 1 slot up to \f$ n \f$, so that a pool
 that has held at most \f$ k \f$ objects at once has at most
 \f$ 2 k + n \f$ slots, and the allocations of blocks are
 \f$ O( \log k ) \f$ while the blocks double.

 A <em>size class</em> serves all the requests between two sizes with
 blocks of the larger one. `SizeClassAllocator` has the class of 8 bytes,
 the multiples of 16 up to 128, and four classes for each power of 2 above,
 \f[
   s = 2^e + j \, 2^{e - 2} , \quad j = 1 , \ldots , 4 , \quad
   7 \le e \le 14 . \tag{3}
 \f]
 A request between two consecutive sizes of (3) wastes less than
 \f$ 2^{e - 2} \f$ bytes of a block of at least \f$ 5 \cdot 2^{e - 2} \f$,
 i.e., less than 20% of it; the class is found from the position of the
 leading bit of \f$ n - 1 \f$ and two more bits below it, with no table and
 no loop. Each class has its free list and a page whose rest is cut a
 block at a time, so that both operations take \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ time (the
 scheme of TLSF and of the allocators of today, by size classes rather than
 by fits).

 `PoolResource` keeps the classes of 8 bytes up to \f$ M \f$ on the pages of
 `PageHeap`, with one free list per page instead of one per class (the
 sharding of mimalloc): a class allocates from its current page, the pop of
 the list of the page or the bump of its pointer, and moves to another page
 with free slots only when the current one is exhausted. A slot finds its
 page by masking the low 16 bits of its address, so that a free is a push on
 the list of that page. After frees in a scrambled order, a single free list
 gives the slots back in that order, scattered over all the memory, while
 the lists by page give them back a page at a time, so that the nodes of a
 container built again are close to each other. `ConcurrentPoolResource`
 gives each thread such a heap of its own, and a slot freed by a thread
 other than its owner goes back to the owner in a batch of up to 32, pushed
 whole on a stack of returns by one compare-and-swap, which the owner takes
 whole by an exchange (see the theory of concurrency for the argument that
 excludes the ABA problem); the frees of one thread to another thus cost one
 atomic operation per batch (snmalloc).

 \section theory_memory_huge Huge pages

 A structure whose nodes are reached in no useful order, e.g., the trees of
 a heap or the buckets of a hash table, misses the translation lookaside
 buffer (TLB) at nearly each step when its memory spans more pages than the
 TLB has entries: with \f$ E \f$ entries a TLB covers \f$ 4 E \f$ KB of
 pages of 4 KB, and \f$ 2 E \f$ MB of huge pages of 2 MB, 512 times more.
 `HugePageAllocator` aligns the blocks of at least 2 MB to 2 MB, rounds them
 up to whole huge pages and advises them to the kernel for the transparent
 huge pages (Navarro et al.), at the price of at most one huge page of
 address space per block, which is never touched; the blocks of 8 MB or
 more given back are kept by their thread, up to 64 MB, for the next request
 of the same size, so that a structure that grows and shrinks through the
 same sizes pays the faults of its pages once.

 \section theory_memory_gc Garbage collection

 In `CellHeap` the cells have two fields, each nil, an atom or the address
 of a cell, and the cells not reachable from the roots are garbage, which
 `collect()` finds by marking the reachable cells and sweeping the others
 onto the free list. A marking by a visit in depth needs a stack as deep as
 the longest path of cells, which a collector, run when the memory is
 exhausted, cannot allocate.

 <em>Lemma 3 (Deutsch, Schorr and Waite).</em> During the marking by
 pointer reversal (Algorithm 2.3.5E of Knuth), with \f$ c \f$ the current
 cell and \f$ p \f$ the previous one, the field of \f$ p \f$ that the visit
 followed holds the previous cell of \f$ p \f$, a bit of \f$ p \f$ telling
 which field it is, so that
 \f[
   p , \; \mathrm{back}( p ) , \; \mathrm{back}( \mathrm{back}( p ) ) ,
   \ldots \tag{4}
 \f]
 is the path of the visit from \f$ c \f$ back to the root, in reverse.

 Going down a field, the visit stores \f$ p \f$ in that field of \f$ c \f$
 and moves on; going back up, it reads the field of \f$ p \f$ that the bit
 names, restores in it the cell it comes from, and moves to \f$ p \f$. By
 (4) the path needs no stack, one bit per cell besides the mark, and every
 field is restored when the visit leaves its cell; each cell reached is
 entered once and left at most twice, and the marking costs time linear in
 the cells reached. The sweep reads every cell, and a collection costs
 \f$ O( n ) \f$ on a heap of \f$ n \f$ cells; a `cons()` costs
 \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized over the cells freed by the collection that
 precedes it, when they are a constant fraction of the heap.

